WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.636 position:50% size:92% align:center
The eight token categories include four zeros.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:06.011 position:50% size:92% align:center
Several threads therefore update the

00:00:06.011 --> 00:00:07.661 position:50% size:92% align:center
same category-zero count.

00:00:07.881 --> 00:00:10.645 position:50% size:92% align:center
An atomic addition makes one supported update

00:00:10.645 --> 00:00:13.532 position:50% size:92% align:center
indivisible. It does not make the block’s other

00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:15.497 position:50% size:92% align:center
operations a collective barrier.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:20.318 position:50% size:92% align:center
With local aggregation, each block clears its

00:00:20.318 --> 00:00:22.808 position:50% size:92% align:center
own four-counter shared array on its SM.

00:00:23.028 --> 00:00:25.477 position:50% size:92% align:center
All four threads arrive at a barrier before

00:00:25.477 --> 00:00:27.527 position:50% size:92% align:center
those counters are used. The blocks’

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.236 position:50% size:92% align:center
shared arrays remain separate.

00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:33.979 position:50% size:92% align:center
Threads make their atomic contributions to the

00:00:33.979 --> 00:00:36.465 position:50% size:92% align:center
block’s shared counters. A following block

00:00:36.465 --> 00:00:38.300 position:50% size:92% align:center
barrier completes this counting

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.661 position:50% size:92% align:center
phase before the merge.

00:00:39.881 --> 00:00:42.875 position:50% size:92% align:center
Only nonzero partial counters need a global

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:44.895 position:50% size:92% align:center
atomic merge in this example.

00:00:46.915 --> 00:00:50.205 position:50% size:92% align:center
Direct global counting makes eight global atomic

00:00:50.205 --> 00:00:53.427 position:50% size:92% align:center
additions. Local aggregation makes eight shared

00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:55.552 position:50% size:92% align:center
atomic additions and six global

00:00:55.552 --> 00:00:57.197 position:50% size:92% align:center
merges for these inputs.

00:00:57.417 --> 00:01:00.285 position:50% size:92% align:center
That changes where operations occur. Extra local

00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:03.092 position:50% size:92% align:center
work and synchronisation still matter, so these

00:01:03.092 --> 00:01:05.183 position:50% size:92% align:center
counts are not a speed measurement.

00:01:07.203 --> 00:01:10.237 position:50% size:92% align:center
Now consider a hot category: all eight tokens

00:01:10.237 --> 00:01:13.204 position:50% size:92% align:center
choose zero. Pause and predict the number of

00:01:13.204 --> 00:01:16.440 position:50% size:92% align:center
nonzero block partials that must merge globally.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.677 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each of the two blocks still has

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:20.253 position:50% size:92% align:center
its own shared workspace.

00:01:26.073 --> 00:01:29.291 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each block contributes one nonzero category-zero

00:01:29.291 --> 00:01:32.375 position:50% size:92% align:center
partial, so the model needs two global merges.

00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:34.051 position:50% size:92% align:center
The final count is eight.

00:01:34.271 --> 00:01:37.416 position:50% size:92% align:center
Shared aggregation combines local contributions;

00:01:37.416 --> 00:01:40.232 position:50% size:92% align:center
atomic operations protect updates; barriers

00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:42.787 position:50% size:92% align:center
protect the phase boundaries. Those are

00:01:42.787 --> 00:01:44.490 position:50% size:92% align:center
separate responsibilities.
